  <title>6.6.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00494</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.6.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e5434944-cd36-4d16-9df1-68f1310fb91d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>To help students’ understanding of key points from the lesson, pose the following questions:</para>
<list>
  <item> What is unique about the first and last terms in a perfect square trinomial? (They are perfect squares.) </item>
  <item> What is the pattern of the middle term in a perfect square trinomial? \((\pm2ab)\) </item>
  <item> To what binomial does the trinomial \(a^2 − 2ab + b^2\) factor? \([ (a − b)^2 ]\) </item>
  <item> What do you call the binomials \((a − b)\) and \((a + b)\)? (conjugate pair) </item>
  <item> What is the pattern used to factor a difference of squares? \([ a^2 − b^2 = (a − b)(a + b) ]\) </item>
  <item><span>Can you factor \(a^2 + b^2\)? (No, it is prime.)</span></item>
</list></section>
</content>
